Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:54:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 41/52] kernel: more sync object fixes (#4294)

* ci: run the latest VitaAutotests

- Pin VitaAutotests ed71e53, which adds the cases of msgpipe_partial_head_order where two waiters
  queue behind a partial head.
- Skip handoff_more. Whether the woken waiter runs before the releaser reads the state is a race
  while threads ignore their core and priority.
- Trim the reason of rwlock_requeue_writers. Waiting writers already go before readers, only the
  requeue on a priority change is missing.

* kernel: fix the counts and arguments of failed cancels

A mutex cancel took -1 as the count the mutex was created with and refused every other negative
count with ILLEGAL_COUNT. Like on a Vita, every negative count now means the init count, so a
cancel to -2 or -100 succeeds and resets the mutex to it.

A reader/writer lock cancel took any flag, so one like 2 or -1 woke the waiters and reset the lock.
It now fails with 0x8002000A and leaves the lock as it was. The flag is checked once the UID names
an object and before its class, so a semaphore UID gets the flag error and an unknown UID the lookup
error.

A failed semaphore, mutex or rwlock cancel left the caller's waiter counts as they were. Like on a
Vita, they now read 0.

mutex_cancel_counts, cancel_calls and rwlock_cancel_order pass. release_errors_waiters now only
differs when the writer its read unlock wakes runs before the count is read, a race while threads
ignore their core and priority, so it is skipped.

* kernel: leave 0 in the bits of an event flag poll that fails early

An event flag poll that failed before checking the bits, like one while another thread waits on a
flag without the multi attribute, left the caller's bits as they were. They now read 0, as a poll
that gets to check the bits still reports the current ones.

poll_calls passes.

* kernel: take a condition's mutex back as a plain wait on the mutex

A signaled condition waiter that waits to take its mutex back reported the condition's UID in its
thread info, and took the mutex back with the rest of its timeout, so one that waited for the mutex
past its deadline gave up with WAIT_TIMEOUT and without the mutex. It now reports the mutex's UID,
still with the condition relock wait type, and takes the mutex back with no timeout. The waiter
returns 0 holding the mutex, and the time left stays what it was at the signal.

cond_signal_all_order, cond_semantics and lw_cond_semantics pass, and cond_relock_requeue and
lw_cond_relock_requeue now only miss the requeue on a priority change.

* kernel: fix what simple event waits and polls write, and waits for pattern 0

A simple event poll that failed left the caller's user data as it was, and a wait that timed out,
was canceled or saw the event deleted wrote the event's user data. A wait now fills copies that
start at 0 and go to the guest when it returns, so callbacks that run meanwhile still see the
guest's own values, and a poll starts from 0. What a failed call doesn't write reads 0, and a
cancel or a delete still writes the error pattern 0x10.

A wait for pattern 0 failed at once with INVALID_ARGUMENT. It now matches no bit, so it lasts until
its timeout, a cancel or a delete, and a set or a pulse doesn't end it. A poll for pattern 0 still
fails with INVALID_ARGUMENT.

simple_event_edges and simple_event_cancel get closer and their reasons are narrowed.

* kernel: implement simple event info, with the attribute and user data it reports

sceKernelGetEventInfo wasn't implemented. It now reports a simple event's UID, name, attribute,
pattern, user data and waiting thread count, and refuses a size past the struct like the other
info calls.

A create with attribute 0x800 failed with ILLEGAL_ATTR. It is now accepted and the info reports
it. Nothing reads it.

A pulse put the user data and the pattern back as they were before it, and a set or pulse of no
bits still replaced the user data. A pulse now keeps its user data and only clears the bits it
pulsed after the waiters had them, so the bits an auto reset waiter took stay taken. A set or pulse
of no bits changes nothing.

simple_event_info passes, and simple_event_edges, simple_event_cancel, simple_event_errors and
create_attributes get closer and their reasons are narrowed.

* kernel: implement sceKernelCancelEventWithSetPattern

sceKernelCancelEventWithSetPattern wasn't implemented. It now cancels a simple event's waits like
sceKernelCancelEvent, then replaces the whole pattern, the create event included, and the user data
with the given ones. A pattern with bits a game may not set fails with ILLEGAL_PATTERN, and the
count reads 0 when it fails. Its syscall takes the user data and the count pointer through a
pointer, which libkernel fills on the stack.

simple_event_cancel passes.

* kernel: fix the events of a timer with several waiters

sceKernelGetEventInfo only took simple events, and a timer's event went to its waiters one at a
time, each checking it once the one before had it. A pulse event was gone by the time the second
waiter looked, and a set event reached the later waiters only as fast as their threads ran, so a
cancel right after could still catch them.

A timer UID now reports the timer's name, attribute, waiting thread count and a pattern with the
create event and, once a set event came, the timer event 0x8000. On a manual reset timer an event
now ends every wait at once with the timer event, and a waiter whose thread gets the lock back
after its deadline returns that result. An auto reset timer's events still go to the waiters in
turn.

timer_event_wakes_all and timer_waiters pass.

* kernel: set a simple event's delete, open and close events

The waits for a simple event's delete event got the event's last user data, an open left the
pattern alone, and sceKernelCloseSimpleEvent wasn't implemented.

A delete now removes the UID first, under the table lock, so a second delete fails and no later
call finds the event. Then, under the event's lock, it sets the delete event with the deleting
thread's UID in the high word of the user data and the process ID in the low word, through the same
walk as a set, and ends the other waits. So on an auto reset event only the first wait for the
delete event gets it. The deleting thread's UID goes through `KernelObjects::remove()` to every
object's `on_delete()`, and only the simple event uses it for now.

An open now sets the open event 0x100 and a close the close event 0x200, each time, with the
calling thread's user data. The event stays after a close. A close or a delete of an unknown UID
fails with UNKNOWN_SIMPLE_EVENT_ID, and an open or a close that a delete beats fails like one that
didn't find the event.

simple_event_edges, simple_event_open_close_bits and simple_event_errors pass.

* kernel: say where a waiter goes behind a head that keeps its place

The comment in WaitQueue::push() only said nothing goes in front of such a head. It now says the
waiter goes right behind it, so it lands ahead of the waiters already behind that head, whatever
their priority.

(cherry picked from commit 5844e21fb6d7259a31ba1f13b98ed1a648170052)
